1
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:39,260
chromatography, or TLC, is a common analytical technique used in organic laboratories. TLC

2
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:44,140
can be helpful when you are trying to identify the components of a mixture or assess the

3
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:51,280
purity of a sample. It is also used to monitor both reactions and purifications. It can take

4
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:56,340
some time to become a pro at TLC, but this video should help to familiarize you with

5
00:00:56,340 --> 00:01:04,060
the basic technique. TLC plates are composed of a thin layer of a polar

6
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:08,900
adsorbent, either silica or alumina, which has been bound to a plate of solid

7
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:15,060
support, generally plastic or glass. In most cases, a fluorescent powder is also

8
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:19,380
mixed in with the adsorbent to aid with visualization, as you will see later in

9
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:27,430
this video. A sample is applied to a TLC plate using a thin glass spotter.

10
00:01:27,990 --> 00:01:32,710
Micro pipettes, such as the ones shown here, can be used and are conveniently

11
00:01:32,710 --> 00:01:38,190
commercially available. However, in many laboratories, TLC spotters are prepared

12
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:44,390
by heating and pulling capillary tubes or disposable pasture pipettes. One benefit

13
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:49,030
of making your own spotters is that these homemade spotters are usually thinner

14
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:53,390
than commercially available micro pipettes. You can watch the advanced thin

15
00:01:53,390 --> 00:01:56,990
layer chromatography video for a demonstration of this procedure.

16
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:04,980
Once a TLC plate has been spotted with a sample, it is developed in a developing

17
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:09,740
chamber that can easily be assembled from a glass jar with a lid, a piece of

18
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:17,300
filter paper and 5 to 10 milliliters of an appropriate developing solvent. Before

19
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:22,060
you get started, make sure you also have a pair of tweezers, a pencil and a

20
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:37,360
ruler close at hand. Assembling the developing chamber is very straightforward.

21
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:42,680
First, slide the filter paper into the jar so that it is flat against the wall.

22
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,600
Next, pour an approximately 8 millimeter layer of the appropriate developing

23
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:55,360
solvent into the jar. Tilt the jar to moisten the filter paper and close the

24
00:02:55,360 --> 00:03:00,800
lid to prevent evaporation. You should end up with a layer of solvent no more

25
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,560
than 5 to 8 millimeters deep.

26
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:07,560
The moist filter paper ensures that the air in the chamber is saturated with

27
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:12,240
solvent vapor. It prevents evaporation of solvent from the TLC plate during

28
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:17,880
development. Once you have put together your developing chamber, it is time to

29
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:25,980
prepare the TLC plate. In this video, plastic-backed alumina plates will be

30
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:30,700
used. For more information on preparing glass plates, you can watch the

31
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:40,200
advanced TLC video. Before you start, there are a few things to remember. Always

32
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:44,760
use a pencil and never a pen to mark your TLC plates. Ink is soluble in

33
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:49,800
organic solvents and will be developed along with your sample. Make sure that

34
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:54,120
you always mark and spot your sample on the dull and not the shiny side of

35
00:03:54,120 --> 00:04:00,700
the TLC plate. Remember, the dull side is coated with the adsorbent. Even though

36
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:04,420
you should be wearing gloves while handling TLC plates, it is important

37
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:08,260
that you do not touch the face of the plate with your fingers. Oils from your

38
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:15,460
skin or other contaminants can adsorb to the plate and affect your results. Before

39
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:19,660
you can apply your sample to a TLC plate, it is important to mark the plate so

40
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:25,910
that you can keep track of where the sample is applied. One way to do this is

41
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:30,030
to draw a straight line approximately one centimeter from the bottom of the

42
00:04:30,030 --> 00:04:34,830
plate. Draw small ticks through the line at each point where you will apply a

43
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:44,790
spot of sample. It is important that the spots are not too close to the edge of

44
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:49,430
the plate or evaporation from the sides of the plate will result in inconsistent

45
00:04:49,430 --> 00:04:54,110
results. It is also important that the spots are not too close together or you

46
00:04:54,110 --> 00:05:00,180
will end up with overlapping spots after you develop the plate. Applying your

47
00:05:00,180 --> 00:05:04,620
sample to the plate is probably the trickiest part of TLC and it may take

48
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:07,980
some practice before you can consistently apply the right amount of

49
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:13,970
sample in a small enough spot. Your TLC sample should be fairly dilute

50
00:05:13,970 --> 00:05:19,050
containing approximately one to two percent of the desired compound. If the

51
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:23,250
sample is too dilute, you will not be able to visualize the spots. If the

52
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:27,170
sample is too concentrated, you will observe large streaky spots on your

53
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:35,750
final plate. To spot the plate, dip the glass spotter into the sample and touch

54
00:05:35,750 --> 00:05:43,780
it lightly and quickly to the plate. Wait briefly for the solvent to dissolve

55
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:56,410
before spotting again. Generally, one to three spots will suffice. It is crucial

56
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:59,890
that you do not leave the spotter on the plate for too long or you will end

57
00:05:59,890 --> 00:06:04,810
up with large diffuse spots. It's difficult to separate mixtures on a TLC

58
00:06:04,810 --> 00:06:09,250
plate when the spots are too big. Try to keep the spots one to two

59
00:06:09,250 --> 00:06:15,450
millimeters in diameter. The smaller, the better. Once you have applied your

60
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:25,280
sample and the spots have dried, it's time to develop the plate. Using your

61
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:33,840
tweezers, pick up the plate and place it in the developing chamber. Make sure

62
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:38,320
that the solvent level is below the spots on the plate. Otherwise, you will

63
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:43,400
end up with your sample dissolved in the developing solvent. Replace the cap to

64
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:47,680
prevent evaporation of solvent off of the plate and try not to let the edges

65
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:52,200
of the plate touch the filter paper. This will disturb the capillary motion

66
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:57,920
of the solvent on the plate. Keep a close eye on the solvent front. Do not

67
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:01,960
let the solvent get closer than 5 to 10 millimeters from the top of the plate.

68
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:07,040
When the solvent front gets too close to the top, evaporation from the top of the

69
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:11,920
plate becomes a problem. The spots keep moving up the plate, but the solvent

70
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:34,100
front appears to stop. This leads to incorrect RF values. When the solvent

71
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:38,860
front has reached an appropriate height, remove the plate and immediately draw a

72
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:45,060
line at the solvent front. This line will be necessary for the calculation of

73
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:53,220
RF values. When the compound of interest is brightly colored, no extra

74
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:58,460
steps need to be taken to visualize the spots. However, most organic compounds are

75
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:04,810
colorless and cannot be seen on a TLC plate with the naked eye. Fortunately,

76
00:08:04,810 --> 00:08:09,570
most TLC plates contain an additive that causes the plates to fluoresce under

77
00:08:09,570 --> 00:08:15,750
ultraviolet light. Certain UV active compounds are capable of quenching this

78
00:08:15,750 --> 00:08:23,830
fluorescence. As a result, they appear as dark spots on the glowing TLC plate. UV

79
00:08:23,830 --> 00:08:28,090
lamps, such as this one, are commonly found in laboratories for just this

80
00:08:28,090 --> 00:08:35,670
purpose. Just place your plate under the lamp, turn it on, and mark the spot with

81
00:08:35,670 --> 00:08:44,660
a pencil. A number of TLC stains have also been developed to aid in the

82
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:49,860
visualization of spots that cannot be seen under a UV light. You can watch the

83
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:54,020
advanced thin layer chromatography video for a demonstration of a few of these

84
00:08:54,020 --> 00:09:02,170
staining procedures. TLC data is described in terms of RF values. Under a specific

85
00:09:02,170 --> 00:09:07,650
set of conditions, a particular compound should always exhibit the same RF. Let's

86
00:09:07,650 --> 00:09:13,730
quickly run through the procedure for calculating an RF value. First, measure the

87
00:09:13,730 --> 00:09:18,410
distance from where the spot started on the plate to where it ended up. Always

88
00:09:18,410 --> 00:09:24,970
measure from the center of the final spot. We'll call this distance A. Next,

89
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:29,210
measure the distance from where the spot started to where the solvent front

90
00:09:29,210 --> 00:09:34,690
ended up. We'll call this distance B. Do not measure from the bottom of the plate.

91
00:09:34,690 --> 00:09:41,290
This is a common mistake that will lead to incorrect RF values. The RF value

92
00:09:41,290 --> 00:09:46,290
is defined as the ratio between the distance the spot moved, A, and the

93
00:09:46,290 --> 00:09:51,330
distance the solvent moved, B. This value is dependent on the polarity of the

94
00:09:51,330 --> 00:09:56,970
compound and the polarity of the developing solvent. Polar compounds will

95
00:09:56,970 --> 00:10:01,730
have lower RF values than non-polar compounds under the same developing

96
00:10:01,730 --> 00:10:08,070
conditions. This series of plates illustrates the effect of solvent

97
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:13,630
polarity on RF. As the polarity of the developing solvent is increased from

98
00:10:13,630 --> 00:10:19,510
left to right, the spot moves further up the plate. The value of A gets larger

99
00:10:19,510 --> 00:10:24,870
while the solvent front, B, stays the same. Increasing the polarity of the

100
00:10:24,870 --> 00:10:32,460
developing solvent generally increases the RF values of all of the spots. It is a

101
00:10:32,460 --> 00:10:37,900
good idea to use a developing solvent that gives you RF values between 0.2 and

102
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:43,440
0.8. This will generally give you the most effective separation when you have

103
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:49,640
more than one compound in your sample. For example, a mixture of two compounds

104
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:54,840
was spotted and developed in hexanes, a non-polar solvent. The final plate shows

105
00:10:54,840 --> 00:11:01,360
only one spot with a very low RF. The same mixture was developed in ethyl acetate,

106
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:06,880
a polar solvent, and once again the final plate shows only one spot, this

107
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:13,380
time with a very high RF. When a mixture of hexane and ethyl acetate was used as

108
00:11:13,380 --> 00:11:19,180
a developing solvent, the two spots were resolved. Notice that both spots are near

109
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:24,900
the center of the plate. TLC is frequently used to compare the identity

110
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:30,300
of two compounds. In a perfect world, TLC data would be consistent from plate to

111
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:34,800
plate and chamber to chamber. Unfortunately, small deviations in

112
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:40,220
development conditions affect the observed RF values. As a result, it is

113
00:11:40,220 --> 00:11:47,040
most convincing to compare RF values of different samples on the same TLC plate.

114
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:52,340
For example, you know that the unknown sample, marked with a question mark,

115
00:11:52,340 --> 00:11:58,780
consists of either compound A, compound B, or a mixture of the two. You can use

116
00:11:58,780 --> 00:12:05,470
TLC to determine the identity of the unknown. You will need two TLC plates.

117
00:12:05,470 --> 00:12:12,690
Make three marks on the first TLC plate, one for the unknown, one for A, and one

118
00:12:12,690 --> 00:12:17,150
in the center for the co-spot. Do the same with the second plate, except

119
00:12:17,150 --> 00:12:27,480
substitute B for A. On the first plate, spot compound A on the center mark and

120
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:40,940
one of the side marks, and allow both spots to dry well. Drain the excess

121
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:50,040
liquid from the spotter onto a paper towel, and rinse your spotter in clean

122
00:12:50,040 --> 00:13:09,030
solvent before switching to a different sample. Now, spot the unknown mixture on

123
00:13:09,030 --> 00:13:16,730
the center mark and the other side mark of the first plate. Repeat this

124
00:13:16,730 --> 00:13:24,540
procedure with compound B on the second plate, and develop both plates. Now, compare

125
00:13:24,540 --> 00:13:29,700
the two plates. You can see that the co-spot with A shows two distinct spots,

126
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:35,340
while the co-spot with B shows only one. These results indicate that the unknown

127
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:43,000
sample contains only compound B. In this video, we have surveyed the basic

128
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:50,580
techniques that you need to know to use thin layer chromatography in the lab. You

129
00:13:50,580 --> 00:14:00,760
have learned how to set up a developing chamber, how to mark and apply your

130
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:16,530
sample to a TLC plate, how to develop the plate in your developing chamber, how

131
00:14:16,530 --> 00:14:25,370
to visualize the spots on your developed plate using a UV lamp, and how to

132
00:14:25,370 --> 00:14:35,610
correctly calculate RF values. You have also learned how to choose an appropriate

133
00:14:35,610 --> 00:14:42,800
developing solvent for your system, and how to compare the identity of two

134
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:50,740
compounds. Remember, this video is intended to help you prepare for lab by

135
00:14:50,740 --> 00:14:56,020
providing a demonstration of the proper experimental technique. It is not intended

136
00:14:56,020 --> 00:15:00,420
as a replacement for reading your lab manual or the supplementary material. In

137
00:15:00,420 --> 00:15:04,300
order to become a great experimentalist, it is important that you

138
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:10,860
understand both theory and technique. Now it's your turn. Good luck!
